[31m-# Multi-Query YOLO-World[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Why multi-query?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-YOLO-World uses CLIP text embeddings to match visual regions against class labels.[m
[31m-CLIP was trained on natural image-text pairs scraped from the web, not on[m
[31m-construction or civil-engineering inspection literature.  Domain-specific labels[m
[31m-like "Efflorescence" or "Exposed_reinforcement" produce weak embeddings because[m
[31m-CLIP has rarely seen those words paired with relevant images.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Replacing each technical label with 5 plain-language descriptions that CLIP[m
[31m-understands well — "white powder on wall", "salt deposit on surface" — gives the[m
[31m-model a much better chance of matching the correct visual region.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## How it works[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-QUERY_MAP = {[m
[31m-    "Crack": [[m
[31m-        "thin line in concrete",   ← sub-queries[m
[31m-        "hairline crack",[m
[31m-        "fracture in wall",[m
[31m-        ...[m
[31m-    ],[m
[31m-    ...[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. All sub-queries across all classes are flattened into one list and passed to[m
[31m-   `model.set_classes()` **once at init**.  YOLO-World's CLIP text encoder[m
[31m-   processes all of them in a single forward pass.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-2. Each `predict()` call runs **one** vision forward pass regardless of how many[m
[31m-   classes or sub-queries are defined.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-3. The detected sub-query label is reverse-looked-up to its canonical class name.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-4. Per-class NMS merges overlapping boxes that were fired by different sub-queries[m
[31m-   of the same class.  Boxes from different classes are never merged.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Adding a new defect class[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Edit `QUERY_MAP` in `multi_query_yoloworld.py`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```python[m
[31m-QUERY_MAP["Delamination"] = [[m
[31m-    "concrete layer separating",[m
[31m-    "surface sheet peeling from slab",[m
[31m-    "hollow sound area on wall",[m
[31m-    "debonded layer in concrete",[m
[31m-    "bubbling paint on cement",[m
[31m-][m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Guidelines for writing sub-queries:[m
[31m-- Describe what the defect *looks like*, not what it *is* ("brown stain on concrete",[m
[31m-  not "ferrous oxide deposit").[m
[31m-- Use phrases of 3–7 words — CLIP performs best in this range.[m
[31m-- Cover different visual presentations of the same defect (colour, shape, context).[m
[31m-- Avoid jargon that CLIP is unlikely to have seen in image captions.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## YOLO-World batching limitations[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Limit | Detail |[m
[31m-|-------|--------|[m
[31m-| ~80 classes max | CLIP's context window can overflow silently above ~80 text tokens total. The default QUERY_MAP uses 30 sub-queries, well within this limit. Monitor confidence scores if you add many new classes. |[m
[31m-| No sequential fallback needed | `set_classes()` accepts an arbitrary list; all strings are encoded in one CLIP forward pass. There is no need to loop over sub-queries individually. |[m
[31m-| Jetson note | Do **not** pass `torch.compile` or `half()` to the model on Jetson — the default FP32 path is stable across JetPack versions. |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Running the benchmark[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-python multi_query_yoloworld.py \[m
[31m-    --benchmark \[m
[31m-    --model yolov8s-worldv2.pt \[m
[31m-    --frames data/test_frames \[m
[31m-    --conf 0.25 \[m
[31m-    --iou 0.4[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Running the tests[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-python -m pytest tests/test_multi_query.py -v[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-No GPU, no model file, and no `ultralytics` installation required to run the tests.[m
