[31m-  <!-- SECTION 1: BIG PICTURE -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">01</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>The Big Picture</h2>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <p>Before writing a single line of code, you need a clear mental model of what the software is actually doing. Think of DRIFT's software as <strong>three separate systems talking to each other</strong>:</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="g3" style="margin-top: 16px;">[m
[31m-      <div class="card card-blue">[m
[31m-        <h4>System A — On the Drone</h4>[m
[31m-        <strong style="font-size: 14px;">Edge Inference</strong>[m
[31m-        <p style="font-size: 13px; margin-top: 8px;">This runs on the Jetson (the small computer attached to the drone). It captures the camera feed, runs the AI models, and sends results over WiFi to your laptop. <em>You don't write this code — the HW team does.</em> But you need to understand what it sends you.</p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card card-orange">[m
[31m-        <h4>System B — Your Laptop</h4>[m
[31m-        <strong style="font-size: 14px;">Backend Server</strong>[m
[31m-        <p style="font-size: 13px; margin-top: 8px;">This is a Python FastAPI server running on your laptop. It receives data from the drone, runs the heavier AI models (SAM 3, DINOv2, LLM), stores everything in a database, and serves the dashboard. <em>This is your main job.</em></p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card card-green">[m
[31m-        <h4>System C — Browser</h4>[m
[31m-        <strong style="font-size: 14px;">Dashboard</strong>[m
[31m-        <p style="font-size: 13px; margin-top: 8px;">A Streamlit web app that opens in your browser. It shows the live drone feed with AI boxes drawn on it, a GPS map with defect pins, a live report panel, and a text box where the engineer types what to search for. <em>This is your second job.</em></p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="arch-inline" style="margin-top: 20px;">[m
[31m-      <div class="arch-box ab">DRONE<br>(Jetson)<br>AI inference</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arr">──WiFi──→</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arch-box ao">FASTAPI<br>BACKEND<br>your laptop</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arr">──→</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arch-box">SAM 3<br>DINOv2<br>LLM</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arr">──→</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arch-box">PostGIS<br>Database</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arr">──WebSocket──→</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="arch-box ag">STREAMLIT<br>DASHBOARD<br>browser</div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 16px;">[m
[31m-      <div class="callout-label">What the drone sends you</div>[m
[31m-      <p>The Jetson sends you a JSON message over WebSocket for every detection it finds. Each message contains: the defect class name (e.g. "cracked concrete"), confidence score (0 to 1), the bounding box coordinates (x1, y1, x2, y2 in pixels), the GPS location (latitude, longitude, altitude), and a JPEG-compressed image of the frame. <strong>Your backend receives this and does everything else.</strong></p>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-  </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <hr>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <!-- SECTION 2: HOW EACH MODEL WORKS -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">02</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>How Each AI Model Works</h2>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <p>There are 4 AI models involved in DRIFT. Two run on the drone (HW team's responsibility), two run on your laptop (your responsibility). Here's what each one does in plain English.</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <h3>Model 1 — YOLO11n (runs on drone, not your job but good to know)</h3>[m
[31m-    <div class="card card-blue">[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>What it is:</strong> YOLO stands for "You Only Look Once." It's a very fast object detection model that looks at a camera frame and draws boxes around things it recognises. The "11n" means it's the 11th version, "nano" size — the smallest and fastest variant.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>How it works:</strong> It divides the image into a grid. For each grid cell, it asks "is there an object here, what class is it, and where exactly is its bounding box?" It does this for the whole image in one single pass — that's why it's fast enough to run at 60 frames per second.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>Its limitation:</strong> It can only detect things it was trained on. If you trained it on "crack" and "rust," it cannot detect "missing bolt" unless you retrain it. This is why we add Grounding DINO on top.</p>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tb">runs on Jetson</span><span class="tag tb">60+ FPS</span><span class="tag tb">fixed classes only</span></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <h3>Model 2 — Grounding DINO (runs on drone, not your job but very important)</h3>[m
[31m-    <div class="card card-orange">[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>What it is:</strong> Grounding DINO is a "foundation model" — trained on hundreds of millions of image-text pairs from the internet. It understands both images and language simultaneously. DINO stands for "Detection In an Open-world" — it can detect anything you describe in text.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>How it works:</strong> You give it two inputs — an image AND a text description. Inside the model, a vision encoder (like a very smart image reader) and a language encoder (like BERT, which understands sentences) both process their inputs and then cross-reference each other. The model asks "where in this image does the text description best match?" and draws a bounding box there.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>Why it's the magic:</strong> The engineer types <strong>"spalling . exposed rebar . rust stain"</strong> (using a dot to separate multiple queries) into your dashboard. Your backend sends this text string to the drone via WebSocket. The Jetson feeds it into Grounding DINO. The model finds every matching instance — without ever being trained on those specific words. That's the zero-shot part.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>What you receive from it:</strong> A list of bounding boxes, each with a matched phrase and a confidence score. E.g. <code>{"phrase": "rust stain", "box": [120, 340, 280, 490], "confidence": 0.71}</code></p>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag to">runs on Jetson</span><span class="tag to">text-prompted</span><span class="tag to">zero-shot</span><span class="tag to">8–12 FPS</span></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <h3>Model 3 — SAM 3 (runs on YOUR laptop — your responsibility)</h3>[m
[31m-    <div class="card card-purple">[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>What it is:</strong> SAM stands for "Segment Anything Model," made by Meta. Version 3 is the latest. Where YOLO and Grounding DINO draw rectangular bounding boxes, SAM draws pixel-perfect outlines around the exact shape of the object. This is called segmentation.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>How it works:</strong> You give SAM two things — the original image and a "prompt." The prompt can be a bounding box (which is what we use — the box that Grounding DINO already found). SAM then asks "within and around this box, what are the exact pixels that belong to this object?" and returns a binary mask — a black and white image where white pixels = the defect.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>Why we need it:</strong> The bounding box from Grounding DINO just gives you a rectangle. But to measure the actual crack length or the area of water damage, you need the precise shape. SAM gives you that. Once you have the mask, you count the white pixels, and using the drone's altitude and camera focal length, you convert that pixel count to real-world centimetres squared.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>The maths for pixel → cm conversion:</strong> <code>real_width_cm = (pixel_width / image_width_px) × (altitude_m × sensor_width_mm / focal_length_mm) × 100</code>. The HW team gives you altitude and focal length — you just plug them in.</p>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tp">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tp">runs on your GPU</span><span class="tag tp">needs bounding box as prompt</span><span class="tag tp">outputs pixel mask</span></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <h3>Model 4 — DINOv2 (runs on YOUR laptop — your responsibility)</h3>[m
[31m-    <div class="card card-green">[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>What it is:</strong> DINOv2 is a visual feature extractor made by Meta. It doesn't detect objects or draw boxes. Instead, it reads an image and produces a 768-number summary of everything it sees — called an "embedding." Think of it as a fingerprint of the image's visual content.</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>How we use it:</strong> During the first inspection of a structure, we store the DINOv2 embeddings of all frames — this is the "healthy baseline." On subsequent inspections, we compute new embeddings and compare them to the baseline using cosine similarity (a mathematical measure of how similar two vectors are, from 0 to 1). If the similarity drops below 0.82, it means the structure looks significantly different from before — even if no specific defect was typed in. This is how we catch "unknown unknowns."</p>[m
[31m-      <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>For the hackathon:</strong> This is a nice-to-have feature, not critical path. Build it after everything else works. It only needs to run on every 10th frame to save compute.</p>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tg">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tg">anomaly detection</span><span class="tag tg">768-dim vectors</span><span class="tag tg">cosine similarity</span><span class="tag tg">build last</span></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-  </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <hr>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <!-- SECTION 3: DATA FLOW -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">03</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>The Data Flow — What Happens to Each Frame</h2>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <p>This is the most important thing to understand. Here is exactly what happens from the moment the drone camera captures a frame to the moment a pin appears on your dashboard map.</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num">1</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">Camera captures a frame on the drone</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">The IMX477 camera is connected to the Jetson via a MIPI CSI-2 cable. GStreamer (a video pipeline tool) reads frames from the camera at 1080p/60fps. OpenCV then receives those frames as NumPy arrays — basically a 1080×1920×3 grid of numbers representing pixel colours. The HW team sets this up.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag">HW team</span><span class="tag">GStreamer</span><span class="tag">OpenCV</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num">2</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">Two AI models run on the frame simultaneously</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">YOLO11n runs on every frame at 60 FPS using TensorRT (NVIDIA's optimised inference engine). Grounding DINO runs every 5th frame at ~8 FPS with the current text query. Both run in separate Python threads simultaneously. The HW team handles this, but <strong>you need to know the output format</strong> because your backend receives it.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag">HW team</span><span class="tag">two threads</span><span class="tag">TensorRT INT8</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num">3</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">Detection results are packaged into a JSON payload</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">The Jetson creates a JSON object containing all detections from both models, the GPS coordinates from MAVLink, and a JPEG-compressed image of the annotated frame. This JSON is the exact thing your WebSocket server will receive. Here is the exact structure you need to expect and parse:</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag">HW team sends this</span><span class="tag">YOUR server receives this</span></div>[m
[31m-        <div class="codeblock">[m
[31m-<span class="cc"># This is what arrives at your FastAPI WebSocket endpoint</span>[m
[31m-{[m
[31m-  <span class="cs">"timestamp"</span>: <span class="cn">1712345678.432</span>,[m
[31m-  <span class="cs">"gps"</span>: {[m
[31m-    <span class="cs">"lat"</span>: <span class="cn">12.9716</span>, <span class="cs">"lon"</span>: <span class="cn">77.5946</span>, <span class="cs">"alt_m"</span>: <span class="cn">12.4</span>[m
[31m-  },[m
[31m-  <span class="cs">"yolo_detections"</span>: [[m
[31m-    {<span class="cs">"class"</span>: <span class="cs">"crack"</span>, <span class="cs">"conf"</span>: <span class="cn">0.87</span>, <span class="cs">"box"</span>: [<span class="cn">120</span>, <span class="cn">340</span>, <span class="cn">280</span>, <span class="cn">490</span>]}[m
[31m-  ],[m
[31m-  <span class="cs">"gdino_detections"</span>: [[m
[31m-    {<span class="cs">"phrase"</span>: <span class="cs">"cracked concrete"</span>, <span class="cs">"conf"</span>: <span class="cn">0.71</span>, <span class="cs">"box"</span>: [<span class="cn">118</span>, <span class="cn">338</span>, <span class="cn">285</span>, <span class="cn">495</span>]}[m
[31m-  ],[m
[31m-  <span class="cs">"frame_jpeg"</span>: <span class="cs">"&lt;base64-encoded JPEG string&gt;"</span>[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num gn">4</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">YOUR backend receives it and runs SAM 3</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">Your FastAPI WebSocket endpoint receives the JSON above. You decode the JPEG image, take each bounding box, and pass it to SAM 3. SAM 3 returns a binary mask. You count the white pixels in the mask and run the pixel→cm² conversion formula. Now you have: class name, confidence, GPS coords, and the real-world defect size in cm².</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tp">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tp">FastAPI</span><span class="tag tp">SAM 3</span><span class="tag tp">pixel→cm math</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num gn">5</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">Save to database and push to dashboard</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">You save the detection (class, confidence, GPS, area_cm2, image path, timestamp) into your PostGIS database. Simultaneously, you push a simplified version of the detection to the Streamlit dashboard via a separate WebSocket connection. The dashboard receives it and immediately drops a colour-coded pin on the Folium map.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tg">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tg">PostGIS INSERT</span><span class="tag tg">WebSocket push</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num gdn">6</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">Every 30 seconds — batch to LLM for report entry</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">A background task in FastAPI collects all high-confidence detections from the last 30 seconds. It sends the annotated image + detection metadata to Gemma-3 (running locally via Ollama). The LLM returns a structured JSON with severity level, recommended action, urgency days, and a plain-language description. This gets added to the report panel on the dashboard.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tgd">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tgd">Gemma-3 via Ollama</span><span class="tag tgd">LangChain</span><span class="tag tgd">BackgroundTasks</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="flow-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-num gn">7</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="flow-body" style="padding-bottom: 0;">[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-title">On mission end — generate PDF report</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-detail">When the drone lands and the engineer clicks "Generate Report," your FastAPI endpoint queries all detections from PostGIS, sorts them by severity, takes the annotated images, generates a Folium map screenshot, and passes everything to a WeasyPrint HTML template. WeasyPrint converts the HTML to a PDF. The dashboard triggers a file download. Done.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="flow-tags"><span class="tag tg">YOUR JOB</span><span class="tag tg">WeasyPrint</span><span class="tag tg">PostGIS query</span><span class="tag tg">Folium screenshot</span></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-  </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <hr>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <!-- SECTION 4: BACKEND -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">04</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>Backend — FastAPI + WebSocket + PostGIS</h2>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <p>FastAPI is your central nervous system. Everything flows through it. Here's what it needs to do and how to structure it.</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <h3>What FastAPI needs to handle</h3>[m
[31m-    <div class="g2">[m
[31m-      <div class="card card-blue">[m
[31m-        <h4>WebSocket endpoints</h4>[m
[31m-        <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>/ws/drone</strong> — receives data FROM the Jetson (detections + frames). This is where all the AI inference results arrive.<br><br>[m
[31m-        <strong>/ws/dashboard</strong> — sends data TO the Streamlit dashboard. When a new detection is processed, push a summary here so the map pin appears instantly.</p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card card-orange">[m
[31m-        <h4>REST endpoints</h4>[m
[31m-        <p style="font-size: 13px;"><strong>POST /query</strong> — receives the text query from the dashboard and forwards it to the drone via the drone WebSocket.<br><br>[m
[31m-        <strong>GET /detections</strong> — returns all stored detections from PostGIS as GeoJSON for the map.<br><br>[m
[31m-        <strong>GET /report/pdf</strong> — triggers PDF generation and returns the file.</p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
