[31m-  <!-- SECTION 2: SOLUTION -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">02</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>How ZeroDefect Solves It</h2>[m
[31m-      <div class="sec-line"></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="callout">[m
[31m-      <div class="callout-label">The solution in one line</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="callout-text">[m
[31m-        <strong>A drone-based AI inspection system where an engineer types "concrete spalling" or "सीमेंट की दरार" into a dashboard — and the drone instantly finds, segments, measures, and reports every instance of it across an entire structure.</strong> No training data. No custom models. No risk to human life.[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="g2" style="margin-top: 20px;">[m
[31m-      <div>[m
[31m-        <p>ZeroDefect has three core components working together. The <strong>drone</strong> acts as the mobile sensor platform — flying autonomous waypoint paths around, beneath, and alongside structures. The <strong>AI stack</strong> (Grounding DINO + SAM 3 + LLM) turns the camera feed into structured defect intelligence using natural language. The <strong>dashboard and report system</strong> turns that intelligence into immediately actionable outputs for engineers and government clients.</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-        <p>The key insight is that inspection engineers already know what they're looking for. They don't need the AI to be trained on thousands of images — they need to describe the defect in plain language and have the system find it. That's exactly what Grounding DINO enables. It's a foundation model that was trained on hundreds of millions of image-text pairs, so it already understands concepts like "rusted rebar," "efflorescence," "delaminated concrete," or "corroded cable" — without any fine-tuning.</p>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-        <p>After detection, <strong>SAM 3</strong> draws pixel-precise boundaries around each defect — allowing us to calculate exact crack length in centimetres and damaged area in cm². This is critical for Indian PWD reporting, which requires quantified severity ratings (L1/L2/L3) to trigger repair budgets. The <strong>LLM</strong> then generates a structured report entry in the format that state PWD departments actually require — in English and optionally Hindi.</p>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div>[m
[31m-        <div class="card card-green">[m
[31m-          <h3>What changes with ZeroDefect</h3>[m
[31m-          <table class="spec">[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Inspection time</td><td>2–3 days → 90 minutes per structure</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Human risk</td><td>High (rope access) → Zero (remote operation)</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Accessible areas only → Full 3D envelope</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Paper registers → GPS-tagged digital PDF</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Defect quantification</td><td>Subjective → Measured in cm/cm²</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Inspection frequency</td><td>Every 5–10 years → Every quarter</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Historical tracking</td><td>None → AI-compared across time</td></tr>[m
[31m-            <tr><td>Cost per inspection</td><td>₹5–15L → ₹80K–1.5L</td></tr>[m
[31m-          </table>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <!-- HOW THE DRONE ACTUALLY WORKS -->[m
[31m-    <h4 style="margin-top: 28px; font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; font-size: 20px; color: var(--text-bright); margin-bottom: 16px;">Step-by-step: what happens during an inspection flight</h4>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">1</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">Mission briefing — engineer defines the inspection</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">The structural engineer opens the ZeroDefect dashboard on a laptop at the inspection site (could be a PWD office vehicle or a site tent). They upload the structure's boundary polygon on a satellite map, set the flight altitude (typically 5–12m for close inspection), and type the defect queries they want to search for: <em>"cracked concrete . spalling . exposed rebar . rust stain . water seepage marks"</em>. The system uploads these as a mission plan to the Pixhawk flight controller via MAVSDK.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-b">QGroundControl</span><span class="tag tag-b">MAVSDK-Python</span><span class="tag tag-b">Mission Planner</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">2</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">Autonomous flight — drone patrols the structure</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">The quadcopter takes off and executes the pre-planned waypoint mission. It flies in a lawnmower/orbital pattern at the specified altitude, maintaining consistent distance from the structure surface via the FC's altitude hold. The 2-axis gimbal keeps the camera perpendicular to the surface at all times. The Jetson Orin Nano runs the full inference pipeline locally — no internet needed on-site. GPS coordinates are logged at 10Hz via MAVLink. An LTE module (Jio/Airtel 4G SIM) streams annotated video to the dashboard if available; if not, results are cached locally and pushed on landing.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-s">ArduCopter SITL</span><span class="tag tag-s">Pixhawk 6C</span><span class="tag tag-s">HERE3+ RTK GPS</span><span class="tag tag-s">4G LTE fallback</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">3</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">Edge AI — real-time defect detection on Jetson</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">Two inference threads run simultaneously on the Jetson Orin Nano Super (67 TOPS). Thread A runs YOLO11n at 60+ FPS via TensorRT INT8 — a fast detector for known defect classes (crack, spalling, rust, missing bolt). Thread B runs Grounding DINO Edge at 8–12 FPS using the engineer's text queries — detecting anything described in natural language regardless of whether it was in any training set. Detections are fused using weighted NMS. Each detection is GPS-tagged by correlating the frame timestamp with MAVLink telemetry.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-g">YOLO11n · TensorRT INT8 · 60fps</span><span class="tag tag-g">Grounding DINO Edge · INT8</span><span class="tag tag-g">Weighted NMS fusion</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">4</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">Ground station — segmentation and measurement</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">Detection JSON + compressed frames stream to the ground station laptop via WebSocket over 4G/WiFi. For each high-confidence detection, SAM 3 runs on the laptop's GPU (even an RTX 3060 is sufficient). SAM 3 uses the Grounding DINO bounding box as a prompt and returns a pixel-precise mask of the defect. Using the drone's altitude, GPS, and camera focal length (pre-calibrated), the system converts pixel dimensions to real-world measurements: <em>"Longitudinal crack, length 47cm, width 3.2cm"</em>. This data directly maps to IRC 22–2015 severity classification: L1 (minor), L2 (moderate), L3 (critical).</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-gold">SAM 3 (PyTorch)</span><span class="tag tag-gold">Pixel→cm conversion</span><span class="tag tag-gold">IRC 22-2015 severity mapping</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">5</div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-line"></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">LLM analysis — structured maintenance report</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">Every 30 seconds, batched high-confidence detections with annotated images are sent to GPT-4o vision (online) or Gemma-3 12B via Ollama (offline — critical for remote sites in India with poor connectivity). The LLM is given a structured system prompt that matches the PWD inspection report format. Output: severity level, recommended action, urgency timeline, and a plain-language description. The system also generates an optional Hindi summary. All entries populate a live scrolling report panel on the dashboard and feed into the PDF export.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-p">GPT-4o (online)</span><span class="tag tag-p">Gemma-3 12B via Ollama (offline)</span><span class="tag tag-p">LangChain JSON schema</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="pipe-step">[m
[31m-      <div class="p-num">6</div>[m
[31m-      <div></div>[m
[31m-      <div class="p-body" style="padding-bottom: 0;">[m
[31m-        <div class="p-title">Report generation — PWD-ready PDF in seconds</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="p-detail">On mission completion, WeasyPrint generates a PDF structured identically to state PWD bridge inspection formats: cover page (structure name, inspection date, GPS bounds, engineer name), executive summary with overall structural health score (0–100), defect inventory table sorted by severity, GPS map with colour-coded pins, annotated photographs of each defect, and a prioritized action list with estimated repair timelines and budget ranges in INR. This document is immediately submittable to the state PWD — no post-processing needed.</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="step-tags" style="margin-top: 8px;">[m
[31m-          <span class="tag tag-g">WeasyPrint</span><span class="tag tag-g">PostGIS</span><span class="tag tag-g">Folium / Mapbox</span><span class="tag tag-g">PWD report format</span>[m
[31m-        </div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-  </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <hr>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  <!-- SECTION 3: USP -->[m
[31m-  <div class="sec">[m
[31m-    <div class="sec-header">[m
[31m-      <span class="sec-num">03</span>[m
[31m-      <h2>Unique Selling Points</h2>[m
[31m-      <div class="sec-line"></div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-    <div class="g2">[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">01</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">Zero-shot detection — no training data ever</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">Every competing drone inspection product (Skydio, Percepto, Botlink) requires custom ML models trained on thousands of labeled images per defect category. This takes weeks and thousands of dollars per defect type. ZeroDefect uses Grounding DINO — a foundation model — that understands open-vocabulary text. An engineer types the defect, the system finds it. Immediately. For any defect. Forever. <strong>This is architecturally impossible with conventional YOLO-based systems.</strong></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">02</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">Works offline — built for India's terrain</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">Most infrastructure in India that needs inspection is in remote locations — NH bridges in the Northeast, dam faces in hill states, power pylons in tribal belts. ZeroDefect runs fully offline: Jetson Orin Nano handles edge inference, Gemma-3 runs locally via Ollama, the dashboard runs on a ₹60K laptop, results save to local PostGIS. <strong>No internet required during the mission.</strong> 4G is optional for live dashboard streaming in urban sites.</div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">03</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">Quantified defects, not just photos</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">Traditional drone inspection gives you a gallery of images. ZeroDefect gives you <em>measurements</em>. SAM 3 pixel masks + GPS altitude = exact crack length in cm, spalling area in cm², water seepage spread in m². These numbers directly map to IRC 22-2015 severity classifications (L1/L2/L3) which determine repair budget allocations in state PWD. <strong>The output is a submission-ready PWD report, not a photo album.</strong></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">04</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">Temporal tracking — catch deterioration before failure</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">DINOv2 embeddings from every inspection are stored in PostGIS. When the same structure is re-inspected 3 months later, the system automatically computes a structural change delta — showing exactly which sections deteriorated, by how much, and at what rate. <strong>This turns reactive inspection into predictive maintenance</strong> — India's infrastructure management goes from "inspect after a collapse scare" to "replace before it fails."</div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">05</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">Safer than any current alternative</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">Rope-access inspection workers in India earn ₹800–1200/day with no fall insurance. Several die each year on inspection jobs. ZeroDefect replaces human access to dangerous locations entirely. The drone goes where humans cannot — beneath bridge decks, across dam faces, inside cooling tower voids. <strong>Zero human access to hazardous locations during the inspection.</strong></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-      <div class="card">[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-num">06</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-title">10× cheaper than current methods</div>[m
[31m-        <div class="usp-body">A standard PWD structural audit by a consulting firm costs ₹5–15 lakh for a medium bridge, takes 2–4 days, and produces a handwritten report. ZeroDefect's SaaS model prices at ₹80K–1.5L per inspection, takes 90 minutes, and delivers a GPS-tagged digital PDF. <strong>The cost reduction makes quarterly inspection financially viable for the first time.</strong></div>[m
[31m-      </div>[m
[31m-    </div>[m
[31m-[m
